WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.100
Nissl and Golgi stains show cell bodies or whole cell shapes.

2
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:11.200
They do not say which protein a cell contains.

3
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:17.800
Immunostaining uses an antibody against one chosen protein, so it reports molecular

4
00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:20.620
identity. It answers three questions.

5
00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:23.140
In which cells is this protein?

6
00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:24.980
In which part of the cell?

7
00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:27.820
And does it overlap with another protein?

8
00:00:28.733 --> 00:00:30.673
First, fix the tissue.

9
00:00:31.753 --> 00:00:36.833
Formaldehyde reacts with amine groups, then forms methylene bridges between

10
00:00:36.833 --> 00:00:37.833
neighboring groups.

11
00:00:38.193 --> 00:00:40.013
That locks proteins in place.

12
00:00:41.153 --> 00:00:43.353
Then cut the tissue into sections.

13
00:00:44.253 --> 00:00:48.033
Open the membranes with a detergent, so antibodies can reach inside.

14
00:00:48.933 --> 00:00:54.493
Then block unwanted sticking, using a protein such as BSA and normal serum.

15
00:00:55.417 --> 00:00:58.357
Now apply the primary antibody.

16
00:00:59.217 --> 00:01:02.317
It binds its epitope through the variable end.

17
00:01:03.217 --> 00:01:05.857
Next comes a labeled secondary antibody.

18
00:01:06.657 --> 00:01:09.177
It binds the constant end of the primary.

19
00:01:10.077 --> 00:01:13.577
A rabbit primary is found by an anti-rabbit secondary.

20
00:01:14.377 --> 00:01:19.597
The label is a fluorophore, or an enzyme such as horseradish peroxidase.

21
00:01:20.377 --> 00:01:23.137
The enzyme makes a colored product at the site.

22
00:01:23.137 --> 00:01:27.717
That gives a permanent preparation, seen in an ordinary microscope.

23
00:01:28.617 --> 00:01:32.517
Image a fluorescent slide with a fluorescence microscope.

24
00:01:32.517 --> 00:01:39.077
Or use a confocal, where a pinhole rejects out-of-focus light and gives optical

25
00:01:39.077 --> 00:01:40.817
sections that can be stacked.

26
00:01:41.777 --> 00:01:47.497
Use several antibodies in different colors in one section, and you see which markers

27
00:01:47.497 --> 00:01:49.337
coincide in one cell.

28
00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:54.360
Read the image as a map of where the protein sits.

29
00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:59.300
Signal in the nucleus fits a transcription factor, such as Fos.

30
00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:05.200
A crescent on the membrane, or signal on a lysosome surface, answers a different

31
00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:10.900
question. Counting stained nuclei needs a threshold and matched settings.

32
00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:15.560
Saturated pixels no longer report how much label is present.

33
00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:20.060
Where two colors overlap, the proteins lie close together.

34
00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:23.100
That is proximity, not interaction.

35
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:29.580
Bright signal means antibody is bound, not necessarily that the protein is there.

36
00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:32.480
So run controls on matched tissue.

37
00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:37.980
Omit the primary antibody, and any signal is background, such as nonspecific

38
00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:39.280
binding of the secondary.

39
00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:44.460
Best of all, stain tissue from a knockout, processed the same way.

40
00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:46.140
The signal should vanish.

41
00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:51.540
Peptide competition, where you block the antibody with its target peptide, has

42
00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:53.720
limits, so use it with other controls.

43
00:02:54.633 --> 00:03:01.153
In the early 1940s, Coons's team joined antibody to a fluorescent group, so the

44
00:03:01.153 --> 00:03:05.233
antigen-antibody complex could be seen under ultraviolet light.

45
00:03:06.273 --> 00:03:11.593
In 1988, Sagar, Sharp and Curran stimulated rat cortex.

46
00:03:12.653 --> 00:03:18.813
Three hours later, nuclear Fos staining appeared in thalamus, pontine nuclei, globus

47
00:03:18.813 --> 00:03:20.373
pallidus and cerebellum.

48
00:03:20.373 --> 00:03:24.813
That made Fos staining a cellular map of activated pathways.

49
00:03:25.767 --> 00:03:34.207
In 1994, Rhyu, Jan and Jan stained dividing Drosophila sensory organ precursor

50
00:03:34.207 --> 00:03:40.547
cells. The protein Numb lay on one half and passed to one daughter cell.

51
00:03:41.607 --> 00:03:48.727
In 2010, Sancak and colleagues showed that amino acids move mTORC1 to lysosomal

52
00:03:48.727 --> 00:03:51.187
membranes, where the Rag proteins reside.

53
00:03:52.187 --> 00:03:56.967
Labeling mTOR and a lysosomal protein in fixed cells showed it.

54
00:03:57.307 --> 00:03:59.567
A stain shows where antibody bound.

55
00:04:00.347 --> 00:04:01.507
Judge it against controls.
